| V: Xenophanes | [Divinity, human perspectives, and knowledge](../presocratics/Xenophanes.lingua#r_7R4H94PKX5S41NDVCJSZE0N88B) |
| VI: Parmenides | [Being and the force of an argument](../presocratics/Parmenides.lingua#r_7HVRAJY7AF3178NEH1N8BF9NPV) |
| VII–VIII: Zeno and Melissos | [Motion, plurality, and argument](../presocratics/Zeno-and-Melissus.lingua#r_5WR6CJM0JV5QPDX2P5177J0YT4) |
| IX: Pythagoras and the Pythagoreans | [Number, order, and explanation](../presocratics/Pythagoreans.lingua#r_2J6KPA7CCD3NY1G0VCAT35Q2NK) |
| X: Empedokles | [Roots, mixture, and transformation](../presocratics/Empedocles.lingua#r_0JDEHV9WCX6YWPPXE64Z26VAMZ) |
| XI: Anaxagoras | [Mixture, mind, and explanation](../presocratics/Anaxagoras.lingua#r_2KMHNE6TG1NF6X3Q4SXMBHGWXN) |

Open the named chapter from the EPUB contents. Greek passages and critical notes precede the English translation in several chapters; the English reading does not require learning Greek first. Fairbanks' spellings differ from some guide titles. Include the edition, chapter or printed page, fragment number where supplied, and ancient transmitting source in your notes: numbering and editorial groupings belong to a particular edition.

Compare a chosen passage with [Burnet's historical interpretation](Early-Greek-Philosophy-Burnet.lingua#r_1DSKX3DB6E343PZHZABGATVK13), examining what each editor translates, selects, or infers. This 1898 collection makes substantial evidence available; later discoveries and scholarship can change a reconstruction.

